 "notes": {
  "what_is_being_asked": "Whether reading a label's two faces at once is worth building. A running copy reads one image at a time behind `_read_lock` (app/vision/local.py), so reading both at once means lifting that serialisation. Each shape here lifts it a different way and is measured against the reader as it is.",
  "shape": "`serial, N engine threads` is the reader as built, one image at a time. `shared engine` lifts the lock and leaves one engine for every thread to call. `one engine per thread` gives each worker its own engine and splits the thread budget between them.",
  "cores_busy": "CPU seconds over wall seconds. 4.0 would mean four cores worked for the whole run, and nothing was left idle for a second read to use.",
  "rss_mb": "What the process held at the end of that shape. Each row is a fresh process running one shape, so the figure is that shape's own memory and not the sum of the run before it.",
  "slowest_image_seconds": "The longest any single image took. It is what a reviewer waiting on one result feels, and it moves the opposite way from throughput when reads are run alongside each other.",
  "how_it_was_produced": "uv run python -m tools.measure_read_scaling --images N --threads 4 --only '<shape>', once per row, each in its own process, under OCR_NUM_THREADS=4 nice -n 19 taskset -c <4 cores>."
 },
